afghanistan's interior ministry says  three rockets have landed in separate  areas around the capital kabul  well they could be heard as afghan  president ashraf hani was  leading eid al-adha prayers at the  presidential palace  one hit behind kabul's most popular  mosque  another behind its largest shopping mall  the thirds  near the ministry of foreign affairs  there's no information  yet on injuries well diplomatic editor  james bayes  is in kabul for us james what more can  you tell us about these  latest rocket attacks and how  significant is this given its  the holiday of eids  well it's very dramatic and it's very  symbolic  it's worth telling you there have been  rocket attacks during eid before they've  been rockets attacks that have landed  in the presidential palace before but  i think coming at this very tense time  in afghanistan  uh it is a symbolic attack i can also  tell you uh that a senior taliban  official  in the last few minutes has confirmed to  al-jazeera that the taliban  were responsible for this attack the  afghan president ashraf ghani  at those prayers that you saw the  pictures of moments  ago at the end of the prayers he gave a  speech  and he said with their missiles they  wanted us to leave but did you see  anyone leave the prayers i think trying  to show that him and his key  government officials were very calm at a  difficult moment uh but  clearly again i think showing the  vulnerability  of the afghan government at this time  and james if the taliban are firing  rockets  into kabul on idaho how is this likely  to affect the ongoing peace negotiations  between the afghan government  and the taliban  well as you're aware there were talks  that took place in doha  ending on sunday and they appear to have  gone reasonably well  but the key thing that didn't come out  of those talks and which the  international community wanted which the  un wanted  was a ceasefire to mark this religious  holiday the talks could not agree  that they simply said that they would  meet again  soon to try and get a timely and just  solution  and would try to prevent civilian  casualties but clearly  for the taliban it's very clear that  they've not wanted at any stage  to have a ceasefire certainly on the  battlefield  they feel that in recent months they've  gained the upper hand and of course if  you have the upper hand on the  battlefield  that adds to your leverage uh in  negotiations  so i i don't think what we've seen here  in the last few hours  is a really big deal but i think it is  symbolic  and dramatic and again shows the  difficulties facing the afghan  government okay  james bayes al jazeera's diplomatic  editor live for us in kabul james thank  you
 

